บ                         6.0     User Interface                             บ
บ                                                                            บ
บ     What use would be made of asynchronous terminal devices, as opposed    บ
บ     to PCs, in the reccomended UNIX technical portfolio ?                  บ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        Standard asynchronous terminals can be utilised in the recomended 
        UNIX technical platform for tasks that invlove large amounts of 
        data input without much processing.  Specifically form filling 
        type applications. 

        The disadvantage of using such terminals for more complex tasks 
        such as word processing,  is that there are no local processing
        or storage capabilities.  This means that each keypress and response
        has to be passed to the host for processing, thereby utililising 
        a certain amount of host processor time.


        Some of the disadvantages of using standard terminals can be 
        resolved by using "Inteligent" terminals. 

        Such terminals incorporate local storage/editing facilities and
        windowing facilities.  Therefore allowing several applications to
        be accessed simultainiously and a certain ammount of processor 
        intensive work to be done locally.

        Although "Inteligent" terminals can process some information,  it
        is limited and therefore still requires an amount of host 
        processor time.

        Using PC's instead of terminals can aliviate some of the drawbacks
        of using terminals.

        PC running a terminal emulator

                running a terminal emulator on the PC allows the user to
                access the host system as if the PC was a terminal. It also 
                allows for file transfer to/from the PC.

                So,  in the case of wordprocessing for example. The user could
                download a file, edit it locally and transfer it back to the
                host.

                This is again limited in that each file has to be transfered
                to and from the host for local processing,  but it does
                distribute the processing away from the host system.  

                Another limitation of operating in this manner,  is that any 
                application that requires "shared" data,  could not transfer 
                this data to another machine for processing.

        PC running under Client/Server type architecture

                Another way to access the information under the control
                of the UNIX system is to incorporate the UNIX file system
                into the standard DOS environment.

                This would mean that all the UNIX files are available to
                the PC and can therefore be accessed via a DOS application.

                The applications running in this manner could "share" the
                the same data and only the file accesses would invlove any
                host processor activity.

                The limitations of such a system, is that the applications
                would run under the DOS environment and could not therefore
                run on the host system.  Also all the applications the user
                requires must reside on the PC.

        PC as work station

                The workstation differs form the client/server type 
                architechture in that,  the PC would run the same operating
                system as the host.

                This would mean that both application and data would reside
                on the host.  The PC would load the application from the host
                and run it locally.

                Both the host and the workstation could run the same 
                application and "share" data.
 
        To answer the question.  Depending upon the processing/shared data 
        requirment of the applications,  there should probably be a mix of 
        all five of the above devices within a tipical data processing 
        environment.
        
