บ    How effective would the recommended UNIX architecture be in providing   บ
บ    the appropriate functionality ?                                         บ
ศออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออผ

        The proposed UNIX architecture supports Primary Graphical 
        Environment (PGE) with the following features.

        PGE provides a graphical, window-based interface into the Unisys 
        System V environment.  The four basic components of PGE are:

	*	X Window System

	*	OSF/Motif Graphical User Interface

	*	X.desktop

	*	Graphical Utilities

        X Window System

        The X Window System of PGE is an X Consortium and X11 implementation 
        of X Windows that controls the network based graphical windowing.  
        PGE provides a full X11R3 implementation of X clients and Xlib.  The 
        X server is based on X11R2.  X11R4 is supported under Unisys System 
        V.4.

        The X Window System architecture is divided into two fundamental 
        parts: clients and the server.  The clients and server communicate 
        using X protocol over standard UNIX network transports.

        An X Server is a program that runs on a computer that directly 
        communicates to a keyboard, mouse and bit-mapped graphics screen.  
        The server allows X clients (application programs) to open and close 
        windows on its display.  Input from keyboard and mouse is handled by 
        the server, and messages passed through to the clients.

        "X" has been adopted by most workstation manufacturers.  Therefore, 
        applications can be written that are independent of a particular 
        manufacturers hardware.  Furthermore, since "X" is a network-based 
        window system, applications can run within a multivendor environment.

        MOTIF Graphical User Interface (GUI)

        The GUI for PGE is based on Motif version 1.0 from Open Software 
        Foundation.  Motif has a distinctive three-dimensional appearance 
        with a style and behaviour similar to Microsoft's OS/2 Presentation 
        Manager, an interface popular in personal computing environments.  
        The components of Motif are:

	-	An Application Programming Interface (API) which includes 
                the User Interface Toolkit and Presentation Description 
                Language; 

	- 	A Window Manager;

	- 	A Style Guide.

        The User Interface Toolkit provides a standard graphical interface 
        layer upon which the window manager is based.  It includes a library 
        of graphical objects, such as menus and forms used in the construction 
        of application user interfaces.  The User Interface Toolkit is based 
        on the X Window System Intrinsics, a toolkit framework provided with 
        X Windows.

        The intrinsics use an object-orientated model to create a class 
        hierarchy of graphical objects or "widgets".  Many basic, composite, 
        scrolled, specialised composite, basic top-level, and specialised 
        dialogue widgets are contained within the toolkit.  The inclusion of 
        "gadgets", windowless widgets that perform specific functions that 
        lower X server overhead, further extends the functionality of the 
        toolkit.

        The Presentation Description Language describes widget resources.  
        It allows developers to create a text file which contains a 
        description of each of the widgets and its resources to be used by 
        the interface.  This description is compiled into a resource file, 
        separate from the application, which is loaded automatically at 
        runtime.  Users and interface designers with minimal programming 
        experience will be able to use the Presentation Description Language 
        with relative ease.

        The Motif window manager offers a standard environment for 
        manipulating application windows.  It is a separate application which 
        allows the user to move, resize and iconify windows.  The window 
        manager is ICCM (inter-client-communication-manual) compliant and 
        supports monochrome and colour windows.

        The Motif style guide is a document that provides a set of guidelines 
        for application programming, new widget development, customisation and
        extension to ensure compliance with the Motif reference appearance.
 
        X.desktop

        X.desktop is a desktop manager with a Motif appearance that was 
        developed by IXI Limited, Cambridge, England.  It is bundled as part 
        of the Unisys PGE.  The release shipped with PGE is level 2.0.

        X.desktop simulates common office and business tasks graphically.  
        Many UNIX commands, programs, files and facilities are represented 
        as icons.  By manipulating these icons, routine operations can be 
        quickly accomplished without any knowledge of the UNIX command line.

        Programs can be initiated by "clicking" on the icon that represents 
        the program or file.  If data input is required for a program, the 
        icon containing the data is selected and "dragged" to the program 
        file prior to initiating the program.

        Nationalisation

        The Motif user interface and X.desktop use the X/Open Native Language 
        System (NLS) standard proposed in the XPG3 portability guide for 
        handling text information.  This makes PGE readily adaptable to 
        different languages or dialects.

        Graphical Utilities

        The graphical utilities provide clients for PGE that allow users to 
        perform many useful tasks.  They include standard X Windows clients 
        such as clock, font utilities and DEC VT102 and Tektronic 4014 
        terminal emulators, as well as providing additional clients that have 
        been developed or enhanced by Unisys.  These include a four function 
        calculator with simulated paper tape output, MS-DOS emulator, a 
        planning calendar, a manual browser and a DEC VT220 terminal emulator.

        PC Xsight

        PC Xsight is an additional product that allows personal computers 
        access to the Unisys PGE.  By using PC Xsight, PCs function as X 
        servers.  Users then have access to PGE facilities and can display 
        client applications through the Motif interface in a windowed 
        environment.

        The Primary Graphical Environment provides the facilities to 
        develop window, icon, mouse and pull-down menu (wimp) capabilities 
        for existing applications in a relatively easy manner.  PGE is able 
        to provide a PC type environment for users of the UNIX operating 
        system, giving benefits in ease of use and simplified user training.

